B6.1 (BLOCKING) — the absence rule refuses a WAIVED gate, permanently, and
the remedy it names provably cannot clear it.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
`waived` is a first-class ProbatioStatus. Everything else in the codebase
treats it as satisfied: lifecycle.ts:204 and :208 let an opus reach `done`
with a waived gate, check.ts:487/495/509 treat it as passed, the web gate
ladder renders it. `verify` has explicit dedicated handling to NEVER touch
one (verify.ts:189, "A gate the studio has already waived is never touched
by verify, even if it has a command in the manifest"). `automatedGateIds`
keys on `kind` alone and the absence rule demands a `tree:` certificate, so
a waived automated gate now blocks its own merge with no exit.

  lab11: clean, fully-certified branch that merges exit 0 as a control.
  Then the tests gate is set, on the branch, to
    { status: waived, waived_by: patron, note: "no test suite for a docs-only opus" }
  and committed there.
    bisellium merge W-900 ...
      -> 'opus W-900's gate "tests" is declared "kind: automated" but
          opus/W-900 has no recorded tree: certificate for it; run
          "bisellium verify W-900" on opus/W-900 before merging'   exit 1
    Follow that remedy verbatim, on the branch:
      bisellium verify W-900 ...  -> "tests: waived (untouched)"    exit 0
      the record is byte-unchanged; still waived, still no certificate
    bisellium merge W-900 ...  -> the identical message               exit 1
  The loop does not terminate. The only escapes are un-waiving a Patron
  decision or hand-editing opus front matter, which this repo's own "Never
  by hand" rule forbids. A Patron waiver is a legitimate, supported state
  for a legitimate opus; the merge gate is the only component that disagrees.
  Scope the rule to gates that are not waived (and, symmetrically, to gates
  the manifest gives a `command` — `kind: automated` with no command is
  schema-legal per Probatio.command?, verify never certifies one, and
  behaviour 28's own fixture is exactly that shape).

B6.2 (BLOCKING) — the A5.3 remedy is still not achievable end to end: it
omits the commit the fix itself made mandatory.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Behaviour 25's acceptance is "the refusal names an ACHIEVABLE remedy". Under
the old code, `verify`'s front-matter write on disk was enough, because merge
read disk. Now merge reads the branch REF, so the bookkeeping must be
committed to the branch or nothing changes — and the message never says so.

  lab9 (the lab8 shape), following the message literally, nothing else:
    merge                    -> refuses, names the remedy            exit 1
    git checkout opus/W-900  -> "Switched to branch 'opus/W-900'"
    bisellium verify W-900   -> "tests: passed ci/W-900-tests-1a94ac6a.log"  exit 0
    git checkout master      -> "Please commit your changes or stash them
                                 before you switch branches. Aborting"
                                (git rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD confirms
                                 HEAD is STILL opus/W-900)
    merge (retry)            -> the IDENTICAL message, unchanged      exit 1
  So step 3 of the message fails and step 4 restates step 1. Nothing signals
  what is missing. With one `git add . && git commit` inserted after verify,
  the same sequence works: merge exits 0, master lands the branch, and
  opus/W-900 is deleted cleanly (`git branch --list 'opus/*'` empty) — which
  is the A5.3 clause doing its job, and it IS safe now. The clause is right;
  the remedy is one step short. Same omission in the ADOPTION.md sentence
  (A6.1).

B6.3 (BLOCKING) — the STATE is now read from the branch too, nothing writes
it there, and the message misdirects.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
`readOpusRecord` supplies both `state` and `probationes`. The brief's
behaviour 26 says so ("the opus record used for the state and certificate
checks"), but no behaviour pins it, no doc mentions it, and no command can
satisfy it: `bisellium done <opus> [--sella] [--studio] [--now]` has no
branch or repo flag, so it writes the checkout it is run from — the trunk,
which is where `merge` must be run.

  lab7b, this PR: trunk copy edited to `state: done` and committed to master,
  branch copy left at `state: building`:
    bisellium merge W-026 ...  -> "opus W-026 is not done (state: building)"  exit 1
  The operator is looking at studio/opera/W-026.md reading `state: done`
  while the tool reports `building`, with no mention of which copy it read
  and no remedy named at all. This will be the very next thing that happens
  on this PR: pass review, `bisellium done W-026` on master, `bisellium
  merge` -> refused.
  Either name the ref in the message ("state: building on opus/W-026") and
  document the checkout-branch-and-commit step, or keep reading state from
  disk while reading certificates from the branch, and say which is which.
  A mutation confirms it is unpinned: M-1 (full disk provenance) is caught
  only by 26/27's certificate assertions; no state behaviour notices, since
  behaviour 4 uses tmpStudio() outside the repo.

Advisories
----------
A6.1  docs/ADOPTION.md, checked clause by clause against the code:
      - "reads the opus record from the opus branch itself (git show
        <branch>:<studio-rel>/opera/<id>.md, falling back to the filesystem
        only when --studio resolves outside --repo)" — the "only" is wrong.
        readOpusRecord falls back on ANY non-zero `git show`, including the
        record simply not existing on the branch. lab15 (opus record
        untracked, .gitignore'd studio/opera/): `git show` prints "fatal:
        path 'studio/opera/W-900.md' exists on disk, but not in
        'opus/W-900'" and merge silently proceeds on the checkout's copy,
        with no signal that provenance dropped back to exactly what B5.1
        was about. (It refused in that instance; the shape is the concern,
        not that run.) Distinguish outside-repo (fall back) from
        missing-on-the-branch (refuse, or at least say which copy was read).
      - "A certifies: dirty:<hash> gate ... is never compared here" — now
        false in effect. lab12: verify --allow-dirty writes
        `certifies: dirty:f00fabb8`, status passed, committed to the branch;
        merge then refuses with 'gate "tests" ... has no recorded tree:
        certificate for it'. Defensible as policy, but the doc says the
        opposite two sentences later, and the message says "no recorded
        certificate" about a gate that has one.
      - the remedy sentence repeats B6.2's omission (no commit step).
      - the state-provenance change (B6.3) is undocumented.
A6.2  automatedGateIds reads the manifest off DISK while the record comes
      from the branch ref — mixed provenance, the same class as B5.1, for
      the absence verdict. The code comment claims "the manifest itself
      (unlike the opus record) isn't what diverges between the trunk and
      the branch here"; it can. lab14: master declares a new `ci` automated
      gate (exactly what W-031 will land) after an in-flight branch was
      verified, and merge from the trunk refuses every such branch with
      'gate "ci" ... no recorded tree: certificate'. Recoverable — the
      rebase pulls the trunk's manifest onto the branch and one re-verify
      clears it (confirmed, exit 0) — so advisory, not blocking. But it
      means adding a gate retroactively refuses every open branch, which
      nothing warns about.
A6.3  M-4 (drop `kind === "automated"`) = 0 FAIL. The scoping is correct as
      written but unpinned; with it dropped, the real studio's `spec` and
      `review` (kind: agent) gates would demand tree: certificates and every
      merge would refuse forever. One assertion in 28 would hold it.
A6.4  M-2 (drop studioRelToRepo's ".."/isAbsolute guard) = 0 FAIL. Harmless
      at runtime (`git show ../...` fails and falls back), so the guard is
      defensive code with no test and, arguably, no job — `git show` already
      fails cleanly. Delete it or pin it.
A6.5  Reds 08 and 09 are mutation records (proved: both PASS against
      739b7e2's branch.ts) but nothing says so — not the log headers, not
      the test comments, not the brief, which labels 22 "(mutation-record)"
      and calls 8-9 merely "extra regression coverage". Round 5 accepted the
      22.log labelling nit because the brief carried the kind; here the
      brief does not. The attribution is only checkable by re-running, which
      I did.
A6.6  packages/cli/src/prune.ts:49 and packages/cli/src/close.ts:22 still
      read the opus record off disk. prune is benign — `git branch --merged
      HEAD` is the real guard, so a wrong state read can only keep a branch,
      never land code. Noting it so the next round does not re-find it as
      new.
A6.7  Carried and still accepted: A4.1 (pull_after_push inert under
      pr.required, documented), A4.5 (a refused merge leaves the branch
      rebased — visible again in lab14, benign under force-with-lease).

On the design call — still endorsed
------------------------------------
Refuse rather than re-run gates, and read the artifact rather than the
checkout, are both right. The absence rule is right in principle too: absence
really was indistinguishable from nothing-to-check. What is wrong is its
scope (B6.1) and the completeness of what the operator is told (B6.2, B6.3).

Verdict
-------
FAIL, round 6. The round-5 blocker is genuinely closed: I rebuilt both labs
and ran each against both trees, and the fix — not the lab — is what flips
the verdict in both directions. Behaviour 22 is non-vacuous, 26/27/28 are
real pre-implementation reds, the brief's 28 behaviours are now readable by
opus.red_evidence, the 08/09 discovery is real, and the gates are honest.

What blocks is the remedy layer and the new rule's scope, not the mechanism.
The absence rule refuses a Patron-waived gate with a remedy that provably
cannot clear it (B6.1); the stale-certificate remedy omits the commit the
branch-ref read made mandatory, so followed verbatim it aborts and then
loops (B6.2); and the state is now read from the branch while `bisellium
done` can only write the trunk, with a message that names neither the ref
nor a remedy (B6.3). B6.3 will fire on this PR the moment it is marked done.

